# Track 4 — E2E round 2: 20 vCPU total budget
#
# NOTE (2026-06): tried bumping issuer 2 -> 4 to lift R2, but it made things
# WORSE (~250 -> ~210). RS256 signing is CPU-bound: 4 issuers at 0.5 vCPU each
# are throttled and do less than 2 issuers at 0.95 vCPU. R2's 5-vCPU overhead
# can't afford 4 well-fed issuers, so 2 well-fed ones is its best. Reverted.
#
# R2's flatness (~250/s, barely above R1) is the real result: at a small budget
# the token-issuance overhead dominates, so the service model is overhead-bound
# here. The advantage shows once the budget grows (R3/R4) and verifiers take over.
#
# | Service              | cpus x replicas | subtotal |
# |----------------------|-----------------|----------|
# | proof-queue Redis    | 0.3 x 1         | 0.3      |
# | snark+token Redis    | 0.15 x 2        | 0.3      |
# | request-handler      | 0.7 x 2         | 1.4      |
# | verifier-selector    | 0.4 x 1         | 0.4      |
# | token-issuer         | 0.95 x 2        | 1.9      |
# | snark-verifier       | 1.0 x 15        | 15.0     |
# | token-validator (x5) | 0.14 x 5        | 0.7      |
# | TOTAL                |                 | 20.0     |
#
# Load with:  set -a; source e2e-throughput-round2.env; set +a
PROOF_CPUS=0.3
REDIS_CPUS=0.15
HANDLER_REPLICAS=2
HANDLER_CPUS=0.7
SELECTOR_CPUS=0.4
ISSUER_REPLICAS=2
ISSUER_CPUS=0.95
SNARK_REPLICAS=15
SNARK_COUNT=15
# Applied manually on each App EC2: docker update --cpus=$VALIDATOR_CPUS ...
VALIDATOR_CPUS=0.14
